---
// "The MVP" on /case-studies/aviation-platform: the loader's scan as a clickable Figma prototype.
// The prototype is embedded live and loads from Figma as the visitor reaches it (loading="lazy"), on every
// screen size. Everything else comes from data/aviation-platform/prototype.json and the four stills in
// public/assets/case-studies/aviation/, which stay on the page as the fallback.
import proto from '../../data/aviation-platform/prototype.json';
import { checkPrototype, contrastFacts, figmaLinks } from '../lib/case-studies/loader-prototype.mjs';

checkPrototype(proto);
const c = contrastFacts(proto);
const links = figmaLinks(proto);
const [labelLead, ...labelRest] = proto.label.split(/(?<=\.)\s+/);
---
<section class="case-section loader-proto" id="mvp">
  <span class="eyebrow">The MVP · clickable prototype</span>
  <h2>Load, or don’t.</h2>
  <p>
    The MVP centred on one decision in the loader’s hand. The loader scans a unit, then a bag, and the handheld
    answers at once: green, load it; red, don’t. It came first because this is where a mistake costs the most. A
    bag in the wrong unit ends up in the wrong place, and an offloaded bag must not fly. The business wanted it
    first.
  </p>
  <p>
    The app is used indoors at the carousels and outdoors on the ramp, so the screens are drawn for both: a small
    handheld, big type, big buttons and contrast that holds up in bright sun. White text on the green screen has a
    contrast ratio of {c.green}, and on the red screen {c.red}. Every result also carries a word and a shape, so
    colour is never the only signal.
  </p>
  <p class="illustration-label"><strong>{labelLead}</strong> {labelRest.join(' ')}</p>
  <div class="lp-live">
    <iframe src={links.embed} title="Loader scan prototype, in Figma" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen></iframe>
  </div>
  <p class="lp-note">
    The prototype loads from Figma as you reach it, and Figma sets its own cookies. Tap Scan unit, then Scan bag, and
    pick a bag. <a href={links.proto} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open it in Figma ↗</a>
  </p>
  <h3>The main screens</h3>
  <div class="lp-screens">
    {proto.screens.map((s) => (
      <figure>
        <img src={s.src} alt={s.alt} width="360" height="640" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <figcaption>{s.caption}</figcaption>
      </figure>
    ))}
  </div>
  <h3>The rules behind the colours</h3>
  <p>Any failed check turns the screen red.</p>
  <table class="decision-table lp-rules">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>When the scan finds</th><th>The loader sees</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {proto.rules.map((r) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{r.finds}</td>
          <td><span class:list={['lp-pill', r.verdict === 'LOAD' ? 'lp-go' : 'lp-stop']}>{r.verdict}</span> {r.sees}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <details class="illustration-method">
    <summary>Acceptance criteria, as examples</summary>
    {proto.criteria.map((k) => (
      <p class="lp-criterion"><strong>Given</strong> {k.given}<br /><strong>when</strong> {k.when}<br /><strong>then</strong> {k.then}</p>
    ))}
  </details>
  <p class="lp-file">
    The Figma file also holds every screen, this table and the design notes.
    <a href={proto.figma.designUrl} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the file ↗</a>
  </p>
</section>
